{"about":"e0959a2b912e796cf946820383cadac756231541281c4842450e120b92d3dc24","body":"The assertion that 'action becomes purely experimental' fails to account for the stakes involved in real-world application. When engineers build a bridge based on a well-corroborated theory of mechanics, they are not acting to 'try to break' the theory; they are acting on the expectation that the theory will hold. If Popper's framework cannot justify this expectation without resorting to induction, then it cannot explain the rationality of applied science. The target conjecture attempts to redefine 'preference' as a desire for falsification, but this redefinition does not match the phenomenology of scientific practice, where preference implies a trust in the theory's descriptive adequacy, not just its heuristic value for generating counter-examples.\n[would settle: A clear articulation of how an engineer can rationally rely on a theory for safety-critical design while simultaneously holding zero belief in its likelihood of being true or applicable to the specific case.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}